KZ3 patch 1078 (SPU pass 61, level speed): a job module that another job loads at its own LS address can be lifted
a second time at that address and dispatched there. KZ3's Amadeus mixer job (job_0127a180, wid 5 jm_amadeus, at LS
0x4000) loads a DSP plugin module (job_0129bd80, header LS base 0) at LS 0x8000 and calls into it through pointers;
the plugin's only lift was at 0x4000, so every call missed and ran in the SPU interpreter (patch 1069): in the K4
level ~10-12 ms per mixer run, ~0.8 of the 5 SPUs (spu_step/spu_decode1/spu_lookup_ovl_exact are 95 % of the
mixer's host samples, patch 1033 profiler). Now spu_overlay_register_located(code, lsa, span, image) registers the
module's bytes, its load address, its size and the image id of its lift at that address; spu_lookup_located()
serves a miss inside [lsa, lsa + span) from that image (exact, no wildcard) only while LS holds the module's
16-byte header at lsa and its instruction word at pc (an SPU's LS outlives the job that loaded the module). Called
after the image lookup in spu_indirect_branch, in the drain resume and in the interpreter's rejoin probe.
KZ3_SPU_LOC=0 disables it (the interpreter path of 1069 again). The lift and the registration are KZ3's
(tools/spu/lift_spu.py LOCATED, tools/spu/gen_spu_register.py kind "jobloc"); host test tools/spu/test_spu_located.sh.
Upstream candidate (general: any title whose SPU jobs load other job modules at a fixed LS address of their own).

Rebased on ps3recomp v0.18.0 (SPU pass 108): the located lookup after upstream's `spu_lifted_lookup` (#420), outside resident spans; `spu_resident_code_owner` added here (was 1123).

